[108.821297, "o", "evt-00015|2026-05-06T00:00:00.005Z|DC01.corp.example|-|DC01$|3|::1|An account was successfully logged on.\r\r\n\r\r\nSubject:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-0-0\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\t-\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\t-\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\r\r\nLogon Information:\r\r\n\tLogon Type:\t\t3\r\r\n\tRestricted Admin Mode:\t-\r\r\n\tVirtual Account:\t\tNo\r\r\n\tElevated Token:\t\tYes\r\r\n\r\r\nImpersonation Level:\t\tImpersonation\r\r\n\r\r\nNew Logon:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-5-18\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\tDC01$\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\tCORP.LOCAL\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x51C3B8\r\r\n\tLinked Logon ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\tNetwork Account Name:\t-\r\r\n\tNetwork Account Domain:\t-\r\r\n\tLogon GUID:\t\t{233a141f-661e-ef53-8c5e-ec52810dd948}\r\r\n\r\r\nProcess Information:\r\r\n\tProcess ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\tProcess Name:\t\t-\r\r\n\r\r\nNetwork Information:\r\r\n\tWorkstation Name:\t-\r\r\n\tSource Network Address:\t::1\r\r\n\tSource Port:\t\t63704\r\r\n\r\r\nDetailed Authentication Information:\r\r\n\tLogon Process:\t\tKerberos\r\r\n\tAuthentication Package:\tKerberos\r\r\n\tTransited Services:\t-\r\r\n\tPackage Name (NTLM only):\t-\r\r\n\tKey Length:\t\t0\r\r\n\r\r\nThis event is generated when a logon session is created. It is generated on the computer that was accessed.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe subject fields indicate the account on the local system which requested the logon. This is most commonly a service such as the Server service, or a local process such as Winlogon.exe or Services.exe.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe logon type field indicates the kind of logon that occurred. The most common types are 2 (interactive) and 3 (network).\r\r\n\r\r\nThe New Logon fields indicate the account for whom the new logon was created, i.e. the account that was logged on.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe network fields indicate where a remote logon request originated. Workstation name is not always available and may be left blank in some cases.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe impersonation level field indicates the extent to which a process in the logon session can impersonate.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe authentication information fields provide detailed information about this specific logon request.\r\r\n\t- Logon GUID is a unique identifier that can be used to correlate this event with a KDC event.\r\r\n\t- Transited services indicate which intermediate services have participated in this logon request.\r\r\n\t- \r\n"]
[108.822276, "o", "evt-01997|2026-05-06T00:01:00.220Z|DC01.corp.example|-|DC01$|3|::1|An account was successfully logged on.\r\r\n\r\r\nSubject:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-0-0\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\t-\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\t-\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\r\r\nLogon Information:\r\r\n\tLogon Type:\t\t3\r\r\n\tRestricted Admin Mode:\t-\r\r\n\tVirtual Account:\t\tNo\r\r\n\tElevated Token:\t\tYes\r\r\n\r\r\nImpersonation Level:\t\tImpersonation\r\r\n\r\r\nNew Logon:\r\r\n\tSecurity ID:\t\tS-1-5-18\r\r\n\tAccount Name:\t\tDC01$\r\r\n\tAccount Domain:\t\tCORP.LOCAL\r\r\n\tLogon ID:\t\t0x51E07A\r\r\n\tLinked Logon ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\tNetwork Account Name:\t-\r\r\n\tNetwork Account Domain:\t-\r\r\n\tLogon GUID:\t\t{233a141f-661e-ef53-8c5e-ec52810dd948}\r\r\n\r\r\nProcess Information:\r\r\n\tProcess ID:\t\t0x0\r\r\n\tProcess Name:\t\t-\r\r\n\r\r\nNetwork Information:\r\r\n\tWorkstation Name:\t-\r\r\n\tSource Network Address:\t::1\r\r\n\tSource Port:\t\t63714\r\r\n\r\r\nDetailed Authentication Information:\r\r\n\tLogon Process:\t\tKerberos\r\r\n\tAuthentication Package:\tKerberos\r\r\n\tTransited Services:\t-\r\r\n\tPackage Name (NTLM only):\t-\r\r\n\tKey Length:\t\t0\r\r\n\r\r\nThis event is generated when a logon session is created. It is generated on the computer that was accessed.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe subject fields indicate the account on the local system which requested the logon. This is most commonly a service such as the Server service, or a local process such as Winlogon.exe or Services.exe.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe logon type field indicates the kind of logon that occurred. The most common types are 2 (interactive) and 3 (network).\r\r\n\r\r\nThe New Logon fields indicate the account for whom the new logon was created, i.e. the account that was logged on.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe network fields indicate where a remote logon request originated. Workstation name is not always available and may be left blank in some cases.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe impersonation level field indicates the extent to which a process in the logon session can impersonate.\r\r\n\r\r\nThe authentication information fields provide detailed information about this specific logon request.\r\r\n\t- Logon GUID is a unique identifier that can be used to correlate this event with a KDC event.\r\r\n\t- Transited services indicate which intermediate services have participated in this logon request.\r\r\n\t- \r\n"]
[108.822573, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@5a136eabef84:~# "]
[113.523662, "i", "cat << 'EOF' > /tmp/parse_logons.py\nimport re\n\nwith open('/tmp/network.txt') as f:\n    # wait, let's write a clean sqlite query instead of parsing\n    pass\n\nimport sqlite3\nconn = sqlite3.connect('/root/data/eventlogs.db')\nconn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\ncur = conn.cursor()\n\ncur.execute(\"SELECT event_id, timestamp, host, user, target_user, logon_type, src_ip, message FROM events WHERE event_code IN (4624, 4672)\")\nfor r in cur.fetchall():\n    msg = r['message']\n    # extract target user and logon type from message\n    target_m = re.search(r'New Logon:.*?Account Name:\\s*(\\S+)', msg, re.DOTALL)\n    target = target_m.group(1) if target_m else \"\"\n    type_m = re.search(r'Logon Type:\\s*(\\d+)', msg)\n    ltype = type_m.group(1) if type_m else \"\"\n    ip_m = re.search(r'Source Network Address:\\s*(\\S+)', msg)\n    ip = ip_m.group(1) if ip_m else \"\"\n    \n    print(f\"{r['event_id']} | {r['timestamp']} | {r['host']} | {r['event_code']} | target: {target} | type: {ltype} | ip: {ip}\")\n\nEOF\npython3 /tmp/parse_logons.py\n"]
[113.523841, "o", "cat << 'EOF' > /tmp/parse_logons.py\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
[113.523863, "o", "\u001b[?2004h> import re\r\n\u001b[?2004l\r"]
